A de novo design strategy provides an opportunity to generate biologically relevant models to understand structure− function relationships using a minimal construct.32−35 With a simplified polypeptide sequence, the local environment of metal centers may be modified rationally in order to investigate the important factors that govern the properties and functions of a specific site. Previously, we reported a functional copper nitrite reductase model embedded in a de novo-designed threestranded coiled coil (3SCC) scaffold, which represents one of the most efficient model systems for NiR in aqueous solutions.36 Prior to that work, there were a few cases of $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{His})_{3}$ sites in de novo-designed peptides with controlled copper coordination.37−39 Although redox processes have been demonstrated in a few systems,40,41 none were fully characterized in both oxidation states. Our system was the first to have both relatively well-characterized, stable $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ and $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ oxidation states that exhibits NiR activity under aqueous conditions. We observed very positive reduction potentials for these systems, which probably reflected the stabilization of the $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ state in a trigonal-planar geometry.36 Initial designs focused solely on the first coordination sphere of copper, yielding modest catalysts. To design a model with higher rates, the next logical step is to explore systematically secondary coordination sphere modifications around the copper. Of course, such studies are potentially relevant to understanding how solely electron transfer centers as found in PHM are influenced by the same secondary coordination sphere modifications. We report herein a series of modified peptides (Table 1) based on the parent peptide TRIL2WL23H (TRI-H) that address how the local environment, particularly charged residues, influence the redox properties and catalytic behaviors of these designed copper proteins. Models of $\dot{\mathrm{Cu(I)(TRI-H)}}_{3}$ and the other peptides used are given in Figure 1.
## EXPERIMENTAL METHODS
The sequences of the peptides used in this work are shown in Table 1, with each heptad repeat given as abcdefg. Variations were made at the $\boldsymbol{b},\boldsymbol{e},$ and $g$ positions of the heptad in addition to the L23H mutation.
General Procedures. The copper−peptide complexes were prepared by adding a solution of tetra(acetonitrile)copper(I) tetrafluoroborate or copper(II) chloride into a (buffered) solution of apo-peptide. Since the $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ −peptide complexes undergo slow oxidation under aerobic conditions, all of the reactions involving $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ or nitrite reduction were carried out in an inert atmosphere box.
Peptide Synthesis and Purification. All of the peptides in this work were synthesized on an Applied Biosystems 433A peptide synthesizer using standard protocols43 and purified and characterized as previously reported.44 The apo-peptide solution was prepared by dissolving purified dry peptides in doubly distilled water or buffer solutions. The peptide concentration was determined on the basis of the tryptophan (Trp) absorbance at 280 nm (ε = 5500 M−1 cm−1).45
UV−Vis and Fluorescence Spectroscopy. UV−vis spectra were collected in airtight quartz cuvettes at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ on a Cary 100 Bio UV− vis spectrophotometer. Emission spectra were recorded on a FluoroMax-2 fluorimeter at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$
${\mathsf p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ Determination for Cu(II)−Peptide Complexes. The deprotonation of $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ −peptide complexes was reflected in the UV−vis spectra. The UV−vis spectra of a solution containing 0.27 $\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{Cu}{-}3\mathrm{SCC}$ and $0.03~\mathrm{mM}$ apo-3SCC [to ensure that $>99\%$ of the $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ was bound to the peptide] under various $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ conditions were collected and $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ was fitted to eq 1. The protonation equilibrium corresponding to $n$ protons can be described as follows:
$$
\mathrm{EH}_{n}\overset{K_{\mathrm{a}}^{~n}}{\Longleftrightarrow}n\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathrm{E}^{n-}
$$
The total molar extinction coefficient at a particular wavelength can be written as
$$
\varepsilon_{\mathrm{total}}={\frac{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{E}}+{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{EH}}}_{n}\cdot10^{n(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}-\mathrm{pH})}}{1+10^{n(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}-\mathrm{pH})}}}
$$
where $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{{E}}}$ and $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{EH}_{n}}$ stand for the extinction coefficients of individual species in different protonation states.
Determination of Binding Constants. $C u(I)$ Binding Constants. The affinities of peptide trimers $\left(\mathsf{p e p}_{3}\right)$ to $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ were determined by competitive binding assays with disodium bathocuproindisulfonate $(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{BCS},$ BCS) or disodium bacinchoninate $\mathrm{\Delta}(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{B\bar{C}A},$ , BCA) as a competitive chelator in $50~\mathrm{\mM}$ potassium phosphate buffer. The competition equilibrium for the titration experiment can be expressed by the following chemical equation:
$$
\mathrm{Cu-pep}_{3}+2\mathrm{L}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{CuL}_{2}+\mathrm{pep}_{3}
$$
where $\mathrm{L}=\mathrm{BCS}$ or BCA. Starting with $40\mu\mathrm{M}\mathrm{Cu(I)-pep}_{3}$ and $40\mu\mathrm{M}$ apo-pep $_{3},$ small aliquots of a $5.00\ \mathrm{mM}$ BCS or BCA stock solution were added into the system. Transfer of $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ from ${\mathrm{Cu}}{-}{\mathrm{pe}}{\mathrm{p}}_{3}$ to $\mathrm{CuL}_{2}$ was tracked from the absorbance at $483~\mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathbf{L}=\mathbf{B}{\mathbf{C}}{\mathbf{S}}$ ( $\mathit{\check{\varepsilon}}=13000$ $\mathbf{M}^{-1}\mathbf{\Lambda}_{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1},$ ) or $562~\mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathrm{\DeltaL=BCA}$ $\left(\varepsilon=7900\mathrm{M}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\right)$ .46−48 Titrations were carried out in triplicate, and the dissociation constants were fitted using the spectrophotometric and/or potentiometric data fitting program Hyperquad 2006 and the reported dissociation constants for $\mathrm{Cu(I)}\bar{\mathrm{L}}_{2}$ .46−48 Titrations were performed at both $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ 7.4 and $\mathrm{\pH\}5.8$ to investigate how the affinities change in relation to pH. At $\mathrm{\pH}5.8,$ the concentration of the protonated form of the ligand BCS was taken into account.47,48
Cu(II) Binding Constants. As reported previously, the Trp emission at $350~\mathrm{nm}$ decreases upon $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\dot{}$ binding to the $\left(\operatorname{His}\right)_{3}$ pocket.36 A stock solution of $\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}$ was added to ${\sim}800\ \mathrm{nM}\ 3\mathrm{SCC}$ in $\mathbf{\mu}50\mathbf{\mu}_{\mathrm{mM}}$ buffer solution. Triplicate titrations were carried out, and the data were fitted with Hyperquad 2006.
X-ray Absorption Spectroscopy (XAS). A 1 mM $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ (TRI$\mathbf{EH})_{3}$ solution was prepared by adding 0.8 equiv (with respect to peptide trimer) of tetra(acetonitrile)copper(I) tetrafluoroborate solution to the peptide buffer solution in an inert atmosphere box. The $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ of the solution was adjusted to 8.5 using small aliquots of concentrated KOH. Glycerol was added as a glassing agent. The homogeneous $50\%$ aqueous glycerol solution was then transferred to a sample cell and frozen at liquid nitrogen temperature. Data were collected at the Stanford Synchrotron Radiation Lightsource (SSRL) on beamline 7-3 with a Si(220) double-crystal monochromator, a flat Rh-coated harmonic rejection mirror, and a 30-element Ge array detector. An Oxford Instruments liquid helium cryostat was used to keep the samples below $10\mathrm{~K~}$ during data collection. Data were collected as fluorescence excitation spectra and normalized to the incident intensity measured with a $\Nu_{2}$ -filled ion chamber. Data were measured with steps of $0.25\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the $\mathrm{x}$ -ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) region (1 s integration time) and $\bar{k}=0.05$ to 13.5 $\mathring{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}$ in the extended $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) region (1−20 s integration, $k^{3}$ -weighted). Energies were calibrated by assigning the lowest-energy inflection point of a copper metal foil as $8980.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ . An initial $E_{0}$ value of $9000\ \mathrm{eV}$ was used to convert data to $k$ space, and the background was removed using a three-region cubic spline. The EXAFS data were analyzed using EXAFSPAK49 with ab initio amplitude and phase parameters calculated using FEFF 9.0.50 Imidazole outer-shell scattering was modeled using phase and amplitude parameters calculated for an idealized $\mathrm{Cu(imid)}_{4}$ structure with Debye−Waller factors defined as suggested by Dimakis and Bunker;51 the imidazole was refined as a rigid group with distances and Debye−Waller factors linked to the refined $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ parameters, so there were only three variable parameters $(R_{\mathrm{Cu-N}},\sigma_{\mathrm{Cu-N}}^{2},$ and $\Delta E_{0.}^{\mathrm{~\prime~}}$ ).
Electron Paramagnetic Resonance (EPR) Spectroscopy. Xband EPR spectra were collected on a Bruker EMX electron spin resonance spectrometer with a Varian liquid nitrogen cryostat at $77\mathrm{K}.$
A sample of $\small\mathrm{\sim}1\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{Cu(II)}(3\mathrm{SCC})_{3}$ was prepared in a $50~\mathrm{mM}$ buffer solution, and the $\mathrm{\pH}$ was adjusted by adding concentrated HCl or KOH solution to the sample. An extra $0.5~\mathrm{mM}$ apo-3SCC was added to ensure that the free $\bar{\mathrm{Cu(II)}}$ was less than $0.1\%$ , and $50\%$ glycerol was used as a glassing agent. The EPR parameters were extracted by fitting the collected spectra to the simulated spectra using the EPR data fitting software SpinCount.
NiR Activity Reaction Rates. The rates were determined as previously reported for $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathbf{TRI-H})_{3}$ .36 $\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}$ ( $\mathrm{~\~}^{\circ}\mathrm{mM})$ was mixed with $0.18~\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{Cu(II)}(3\mathrm{SCC)}$ and $0.09\mathrm{mM}$ apo-3SCC [to ensure that over $99\%$ of the $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ was bound to the peptide]. The reaction was initiated by adding 6 equiv of sodium ascorbate (NaAsc) with respect to $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ . The consumption of NaAsc was monitored by UV−vis spectroscopy. The control was a mixture of $30\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NaNO}_{2}$ and 0.09 mM apo-3SCC with the same amount of NaAsc. The rates of the reaction were calculated as 2 times the difference in rates of NaAsc consumption (decrease of absorbance at $251~\mathrm{nm}$ ) for the sample and the control. The production of nitric oxide was examined using the method previously reported.36
## RESULTS
$\mathsf{p}K_{\mathsf{a}}$ of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ −Peptide Complexes. For the parent peptide TRI-H, the appearance of a broad band with a maximum absorbance at $644~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ was observed as the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ of a $\mathbf{Cu(II)(TRI-H)}_{3}$ solution was increased from 2.92 to 5.52 (Figure 2A). This spectrum has previously been suggested to be due to $\mathrm{Cu(II)(Hi\bar{s})}_{3}(\mathrm{OH}_{2})_{1-2}.$ −2.36 When the pH was raised above 7.6, a new set of spectra containing an isosbestic point at $571~\mathrm{nm}$ was observed (Figure 2B). The absorbance at $644~\mathrm{{nm}}$ decreased while a new band at ${514}\mathrm{nm}$ grew in intensity. From a fit of the absorbance at $644\ \mathrm{nm}$ to eq 1, a $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}^{\mathrm{w}}$ value corresponding to the changes in the absorption spectra described above was extracted (Table 2); this value corresponds to a one-proton deprotonation process.
aThe two $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ values correspond to two different deprotonation processes. $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}^{\textrm{E}}$ is associated with $\mathrm{Glu}22$ and $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}^{\mathrm{~w~}}$ is associated with the $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ -bound water.
Interestingly, the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ titration of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Cu(II)}(\mathbf{TRI-EH})_{3}}\end{array}$ resulted in $\mathsf{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}$ values distinctively different from that of the parent peptide (Table 2). Again, in the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ region $2.97{-}5.57.$ , the increase in the absorbance at $659\mathrm{nm}$ is indicative of binding of copper to the peptide and is reminiscent of the parent peptide (Figure S1 in the Supporting Information). However, when the $\mathrm{pH}$ was raised from 5.33 to 7.87, the absorbance at $659~\mathrm{nm}$ decreased slightly (Figure 3), resulting in a $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}^{\mathrm{E}}$ value of 6.33.
This deprotonation event fitted best to a 1.65(23) proton process (Figure S1B), which suggested that the nature of this deprotonation might be different from the one for $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ (TRI$\mathbf{H})_{3}$ . Similar effects were also observed for $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ (TRI$\mathbf{EHK}24\mathrm{E})_{3}$ (Figure S2). The $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)\:\:\ d{-}d}$ band evolved differently upon $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ increase for different peptides within the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ region of interest to us (Figure 4). In summary, we observed the appearance of a $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ d−d band when $\dot{\mathrm{Cu(II)}}$ was bound to different peptides, and the transformations of the spectra were $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ -dependent because of various deprotonation events.
$\mathsf{c u(l)}$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ Affinities, Free Energies of Binding, and Calculated Reduction Potentials. Dissociation constants for the complexation of either $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ or $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ at $\mathrm{pH}5.8$ and 7.4 for each of the peptides studied in this work are provided in Table 3. The first point to recognize is that the reversal of the position of the Lys and Glu groups in going from TRI-H to TRI-EHE27K leads to a marked change in the $\mathrm{\bar{C}u(I)}$ affinity (a factor of 100 in favor of the reversed peptide), while the corresponding $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ affinities are higher for the original parent peptide. In an effort to isolate the effects, peptides solely with modifications at position 22, which is much closer to the copper site than position 27 (Figure 1), were examined. At $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ 7.4, for the series TRI-EH, TRI-H $\mathrm{K}22\mathrm{Q},$ and TRI-H, corresponding to $\Delta_{\mathrm{charge}}~=~-6,~-3$ , and 0, respectively, the $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ affinity changed only modestly (a factor of 5 less stable for TRI- $\mathbf{\cdotHK}22\mathbf{Q}$ and essentially identical for TRI-EH) and the $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ affinities were essentially invariant. At $\mathrm{\pH}\ 5.8\$ the mutated peptides TRI-HK22Q and TRI-EH have very similar affinities and reduction potentials (Table 4), although they have higher $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ affinities and lower $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ affinities compared to the parent peptide TRI-H. It is worth noticing that TRI-H behaves quite differently compared to the mutated peptides at $\mathrm{\pH\}5.8,$ possibility because of the lower $\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}^{\prime}\{$ s of the mutated peptides; however, it appears that $\Delta_{\mathrm{charge}}$ of the residues at position 22 (one residue above the binding site, His23) does not have a dramatic influence on the affinities and redox properties of the peptide. On the basis of these observations, we chose to examine a series of peptide substitutions below the copper-binding site (positions 24 and 27) using the K22Ederived peptides, as this would allow for the widest range in $\Delta_{\mathrm{charge}}$ (from 0 to $-12,$ ).
As shown in Figure 5A, the $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ affinities for the series of peptides containing a K22E substitution are related to the total charge around the copper binding site at both $\mathrm{pH}5.8$ and pH 7.4. As the total negative charge is increased, going from Δcharge $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 0$ (TRI-EHE27K) to $\Delta_{\mathrm{charge}}~=~-12$ (TRI-EHK24E), the $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ affinity decreased by about 2 orders of magnitude under both $\mathrm{\pH}$ conditions. However, the $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ affinities seemed to be relatively immune to the change in total charge. As shown in Figure 5B, at both $\mathrm{\pH\}5.8$ and $\mathrm{\pH\7.4}$ , the change in $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ affinities toward the peptides was minimal at a particular $\mathrm{\pH}$ across the series of peptides with different total charges. It is interesting to note that unlike $\mathtt{C u(I)}.$ , whose affinities remained essentially the same for a particular peptide at different $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ with dissociation constants ranging from picomolar to femtomolar, $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ was destabilized by at least 2 orders of magnitude when the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ decreased from 7.4 to 5.8, as shown in Figure 5B. The difference between $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ and $\mathtt{C u}(\mathtt{I I})$ can be described more clearly when we compare the free energies of binding (section 4 in the Supporting Information) at a particular $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ (Figure 6).
While reduction potentials for metalloproteins and small molecules are often determined by direct electrochemical methods, one may extract the same information by determining the protein binding affinities of the metals in both oxidation states and applying the Nernst equation. This approach has the added benefit that it allows one to track directly the behavior of interest (e.g., reaction rates) not only against the reduction potential but also to see whether correlations associated with a specific oxidation level exist. The reduction potentials of the $\bar{\mathrm{Cu(II)/Cu(I)}}\mathrm{.}$ −peptide systems were calculated using the methodology shown in Scheme 1 and eq $^{2,}$ where the value $E_{\mathrm{Cu,aq}}^{\circ}=0.159\mathrm{~V~}$ vs NHE was utilized (Table 4).36,52 Since the free energy of binding for $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ varies with amino acid substitution more than that of $\operatorname{Cu}(\operatorname{II})$ (Figure 6), the change in the calculated reduction potential is dominated by the variation of the $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ affinity. This is true under both $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ conditions; however, the change in potential between $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ conditions is due to the stabilization of $\mathsf{\bar{C}u}(\mathrm{II})$ upon acidification. The linear regressions for the trends following amino acid substitutions resulted in two parallel lines, the distance between which was about $100~\mathrm{{mV}}$ (Figure 7). This value corresponds to a $1\mathrm{H}^{+}$ per electron change in potential, which is consistent with the difference of $1.6~\mathrm{pH}$ units for these measurements.
