E_{\mathrm{Cu(pep)}_{3}}^{\circ}=E_{\mathrm{Cu,aq}}^{\circ}-\frac{2.303R T}{n F}\log\frac{K_{\mathrm{d,Cu(I)(pep)}_{3}}}{K_{\mathrm{d,Cu(II)(pep)}_{3}}}
$$
XAS of $C_{u(l)}({\sf T R I-E H})_{3}$ . The XANES spectra of $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ (TRI$\mathbf{EH})_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Cu(I)(TRI\cdotH)}_{3}$ (Figure 8A) both show a modestintensity peak at ${\sim}8983\ \mathrm{~eV};$ ; this is typical of $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ in an approximately trigonal environment.53 The near identity of these spectra shows that there are only minor structural differences between the copper sites. The EXAFS data for $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{Cu(I)}(\mathbf{TRI-EH})_{3}}\end{array}$ (Figure 8B,C) support this structural interpretation. The Fourier transform is dominated by a peak at $R\approx1.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ typical of $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ or $\mathrm{Cu-O}$ nearest-neighbor scattering, with pronounced outer-shell scattering typical of rigid ligands such as histidine. Quantitative fitting (Table 5) confirmed this structure. The nearest-neighbor distance of 1.93 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ is typical of three-coordinate $\mathtt{C u(I)}_{\mathrm{\#}}$ , and the outer-shell scattering can be modeled using three imidazole ligands; these results are quantitatively consistent with structural parameters reported previously for $\mathrm{Cu(I)(TRI\cdotH)}_{3}$ .36 Attempts to fit the data with a $\mathrm{Cu-O}$ shell in addition to or in place of one of the Cu−imidazole interactions did not give any improvement in the fit (Table S6 in the Supporting Information).
EPR Analyses of Cu(II)−Peptide Complexes. EPR spectra of $\mathrm{Cu(II)(TRI-H)}_{3}$ were collected from $\mathrm{pH}~5.87\$ to $\mathrm{pH}~9.14,$ and they showed a transition of $A_{\parallel}$ and $g_{\parallel}$ from $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ 7.80 to $\mathsf{p H9.l4}$ (section 3 in the Supporting Information). The EPR parameters of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{Cu(II)(TRI-EHE27K)}_{3},}\end{array}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Cu(II)}(\mathrm{TRI-EH})_{3},}\end{array}$ and $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{Cu(II)}(\mathbf{TRI-EHK}24\mathbf{E})_{3}}\end{array}$ at both $\mathrm{pH}5.8$ and 7.4 were in the same range as those of the parent peptide complex (Table 6). Specifically, $g_{\perp}$ is smaller than $g_{\parallel}$ and $A_{\parallel}$ is in the range of $17-$ $19~\mathrm{mT}$ for all of these complexes, indicative of a type 2 copper center with a high coordination number (4 or 5), which is consistent with the observed $\mathrm{d{-}d}$ bands in the UV−vis spectra.
NiR Activity. The Cu−peptides exhibited NiR activity with different rates (Figure 9), all of which are pH-dependent. Specifically, as the total negative charge was increased from 0 to −12, the rates of the reaction increased about 4-fold. Moreover, the rates are also correlated to the calculated reduction potentials at $\mathrm{\ttpH}5.8$ (Figure 10): as the calculated reduction potentials increase, the rates decrease. The turnover numbers (TONs) for these reactions, expressed as moles of electrons per mole of copper, were obtained. The TONs in $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ are linearly associated with the local negative charge (Figure S6). As $\Delta_{\mathrm{charge}}$ increased from 0 (TRI-EHE27K) to $-12$ (TRI-EHK24E), the TONs at $\mathrm{pH}5.8$ increased by one. For all of the NiR reactions, we did not observe any degradation of the enzyme $1.5\mathrm{~h~}$ after the reaction was initiated. Nitric oxide production was confirmed for the peptides showing the lowest and highest rates $\left[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{TRI-EHE}27\mathrm{K})_{3}\right.$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathbf{TRI-EHK}24\mathrm{E})_{3},$ respectively] at $\mathrm{pH}5.8$ (section 8 in the Supporting Information).
## DISCUSSION
The environment that surrounds redox sites plays a critical role in determining the redox potentials and pathways for electron flow in proteins. However, in most cases, multiple factors exert influence on the redox potential simultaneously, complicating the understanding of the structure−function relationship of a particular system. Conceptually, we can think of the energy associated with the redox process as the sum of the innersphere contributions, which directly point to the energy needed
